 ## Overview
-CSG Model Voxelization is a hardware-accelerated algorithm that generates a binary voxelization of a Constructive Solid Geometry (CSG) model by encoding primitive classifications into frame buffer pixel colors and using logical operations to compute the final CSG classification implicitly via a Point-Classification Map (PCM) [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently converting complex CSG trees into discrete volumetric data for applications like volume rendering and layered manufacturing [S1]. Unlike previous hardware-accelerated methods that required intermediate textures for each Boolean operation node, this method computes boolean operations in image space using frame buffer pixel maps, resulting in faster performance and avoiding hardware restrictions on blending function combinations [S1].
+CSG Model Voxelization is a hardware-accelerated algorithm that generates a binary voxelization of a Constructive Solid Geometry (CSG) model by encoding primitive classifications into frame buffer pixel colors and using logical operations to compute the final CSG classification implicitly via a Point-Classification Map (PCM) [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently converting complex CSG trees into discrete volume data for applications like volume rendering, collision detection, and layered manufacturing [S1]. Unlike previous hardware-accelerated methods that required intermediate textures for each Boolean operation node [S1], this method computes boolean operations in image space using GPU blending and logical operations, resulting in faster performance and avoiding hardware restrictions on blending function combinations [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method voxelize CSG nodes into worksheets and performs boolean operations in image space using GPU blending [S2, unofficial]. It operates slice-by-slice (Z-plane by Z-plane), rendering primitives into a frame buffer where each bit of the pixel color represents the inclusion of a specific primitive [S1].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A CSG tree with $n$ leaf nodes (primitives), where each primitive is either a geometric boundary surface or a volume data set.
+*   **Output:** A binary voxel volume stored in texture memory, where each voxel indicates whether the corresponding point is inside or outside the final CSG model.
 
